WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:09.400
 Hey, Devon Sampson here. I'm really excited to announce the sixth season of Delicious Revolution. We're calling it The Movement Builders.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:23.740
 Just about every one of the 50 episodes we've done so far touches on movement. I'm thinking of our conversations with farmer organizers like Katie Hackmeyer, Joy Smith, Jim Cochran, to artist organizers like Amanda Eicher, Alexa Walsh, Amy Franceschini.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:31.730
 I'm thinking about activist chefs like Jessica Prentice and the People's Kitchen Collective, and activist artisans like David Asher and Sandor Katz,

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:38.730
 and activist restaurateurs like Emma Rosenbush. All of these, and many more that I just don't have time to call out by name here,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:44.780
 make up this kind of living archive of a diverse movement around food. Listening back on these recordings,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:52.380
 I feel like I'm listening in on many lifetimes of experience about building movements. And it feels to me like movement building is in the air.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:01:00.380
 We started Delicious Revolution well before Trump and this rise of fascist politics, but like a lot of people, I think this absurd and terrifying year

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:07.410
 reminded us of the importance of building strong movements. Or maybe what I want to say is it kicked me hard towards something that I already knew,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:14.410
 but maybe I've forgotten a little bit. That the way to get through this, not just to survive, but to flourish despite this,

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:19.410
 has a lot to do with building strong, diverse movements for collective liberation.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:27.770
 And I have some questions about the food movement. Like, what is it? Who is it? And is it really a movement?

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:33.770
 A movement, I know, is more than just a collection of nonprofit organizations or a change in consumer preferences.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:39.790
 It often feels to me like there's something more to this thing that we call the food movement, but I'm not so sure.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.780
 And to be honest, I've never been quite sure if I'm a part of it or not.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:50.100
 So maybe there's more important questions. Like, how do we build a movement, or maybe it's many movements,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.100
 that are capable of bringing this fair, sustainable food system we envision into this complicated world.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:02.220
 And the people we've recorded so far have such diverse approaches to making a change and such unique contributions to this vision.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.220
 They don't all agree. I think Chelsea, my co-host, put it best.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:11.220
 She says she doesn't want to be part of a movement where coming together means that we agree on everything.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.360
 She doesn't want to work towards speaking with one voice.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.360
 She wants to be part of a movement that sounds like a chorus of different voices.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.610
 So I think it's time to take movement building head on this season.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.860
 We'll bring you interviews with organizers and activists and get deep into what it means to build a movement.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.890
 The season is going to sound a little bit different from the previous ones.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.920
 Instead of producing an entire season at once and releasing an interview every couple of weeks,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.920
 we're going to produce one episode at a time and bring you that freshly recorded episode once a month.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.300
 I'm going to do a little less editing too, so you might notice that they sound a little bit rougher.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.750
 I know a lot of you listening are movement builders.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.750
 If you want to talk about movement, please get in touch.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.750
 All our info is on the website, deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.750
 And if you happen to be headed to Ecofarm conference this year, I'll be there.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:57.750
 Please do say hi.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:03.740
 And we'll be at the AAGs, the Association for American Geographers conference in New Orleans, this April too.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.770
 We're going to start things off this season with a short interview I recorded last year

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:12.770
 with farmer organizer Elizabeth Mpofu, chairperson of Zimbabwe's Smallholder Farmers Forum

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:19.770
 and the general coordinator of La Via Campesina, the global network of more than 164 peasant organizations.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.770
 She's a truly amazing organizer.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.950
 I spoke to her about her story and about this message from a talk that stayed with me,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.950
 which I think is at the heart of what movement building is.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.230
 She said nothing for us, without us.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:39.230
 Meaning, in this case Peasant Farmers, we don't want anything done for us without us as equal participants.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.540
 Anyway, she says it much better than I can, so look for that interview in the podcast feed soon.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:53.890
 Delicious Revolution is hosted and produced by Chelsea Wills and me, Devin Sampson.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:04:00.890
 Find it on radio stations KWMR and KOWS in coastal California and anywhere you get podcasts.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:07.090
 If you need more details on how to listen, we have them on our website, deliciousrevolutionshow.com,

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.270
 along with photos and more information about everyone we interview.

